<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Regolini-Galassi tomb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 675–650 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Regolini-Galassi tomb is an Etruscan chamber tomb in the Sorbo necropolis of Caere (Cerveteri), found intact in 1836 by the priest Alessandro Regolini and General Vincenzo Galassi. Dating from about 675–650 BCE, it held gold jewellery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze vessels and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, showing the wealth of the Orientalising period. The finds are in the Gregorian Etruscan Museum of the Vatican.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb.jpg" alt="Regolini-Galassi tomb" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_fibula_d%27oro_con_staffa_a_disco,_da_tomba_regolini-galassi_di_cerveteri,_675-650_ac._ca.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tomb is one of the wealthiest Etruscan family tombs of Caere, an ancient city about 50 to 60 kilometres north-northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its architecture and contents show that it was built by a wealthy family of the city. Two burial chambers lie on either side of a corridor 37 metres long and 1.8 metres wide. The lower part is cut into the tufa rock, while the upper part is built of square stone blocks, each course projecting over the one below to form an overhang. The whole structure lies under a tumulus 46 metres across, which gives it the face of a monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The excavators found a royal woman buried in the end cell and a cremated man in the right-hand cell. The body of a third person lay on a bronze bed in an annex chamber, and who this was has never been explained. The grave goods included gold jewels, silverware, gilded and bronze ware, bronze cauldrons and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>. Several pieces carry seventh-century BCE Villanovan decorative motifs, among them a great fibula with five small lions striding across its surface and a plaque 25 centimetres long decorated with animals of Eastern origin.</p><p>The fibula has been praised for the mastery of its technique, as have the Phoenician metal bowls. The Orientalizing influences in the tomb fuse Etruscan customs with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the Eastern Mediterranean. The many materials used, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold among them, show the importance of mineral wealth to a region where Villanovan settlements grew from poor farming villages into thriving cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>General Vincenzo Galassi, a senior official of the papal army, and Alessandro Regolini, archpriest of Cerveteri, had both opened and excavated tombs around Caere before they found this one undisturbed in 1836. After the excavation the antiquities were kept under guard in a room of Galassi's residence, and they were later sold to the Vatican. Luigi Grifi published the contents in detail in 1841.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Regolini-Galassi tomb” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealth from metals, farming and trade created an aristocracy whose princely tombs of the Orientalising period, from about 720 to 580 BCE, were filled with gold, silver, bronze and ivory in styles from the eastern Mediterranean, as in the <b>Regolini-Galassi tomb</b> at Caere of about 675–650 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Villanovan villages united into a city during the 8th century BCE, and by the 7th century the princely <b>Regolini-Galassi tomb</b> shows great wealth from metals and long-distance trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/caere">Caere</a></li></ul></section>
